Yes. Looking at it as a human scene rather than merely asking “does it look cool?”, it breaks in several places. The problem is not that it is fantasy. Fantasy can be perfectly coherent. The problem is that the image changes its rules from object to object and sometimes within the same object.

The biggest offender is the giant war horn in the upper-right. It starts as a believable curved brass tube, but as it reaches the boar head the geometry stops behaving like a manufactured horn and starts behaving like an animal/sculpture without clearly choosing either ontology. The boar head has tusks, teeth, an eye, ornamental metal anatomy, and a mouth that is apparently also the bell of the horn. That’s permissible fantasy—but then the tube-to-head transition should have a physically intelligible joint, bore, wall thickness, and sound path. Instead the model has essentially morphed one visual concept into another.

There are several other failures of the same kind:

* The horn is enormous and apparently suspended in space. There is no mount, bearer, chain, stand, or other force explaining why it occupies that position. “Magic floating horn” could work, but nothing signals that magic is operating.
* Its scale relative to the pouch, flags, trees, and mountains is ambiguous. It could be a 40-foot monument, a foreground object, or decorative graphic art. The scene doesn’t establish which.
* The pouch sits on rock, but the contact geometry is weak. Its lower edge feels partially pasted onto the terrain rather than deforming, resting, or casting a convincing contact shadow.
* The gummies are similarly composited. They’re photorealistic objects sitting in a painterly/fantasy landscape, but their shadows and scale don’t fully marry them to the ground.
* The right-hand “FAST-ACTING ENERGY” medallion is especially revealing. Is it a physical shield standing in the battlefield, or a flat advertising badge overlaid on the image? It has dimensional styling like an object but behaves spatially like UI.
* The banners, spears, terrain, horn, pouch, badge, and headline occupy several different representational layers, yet the image doesn’t consistently tell your eye which things belong to the physical world and which belong to the graphic-design layer.
* Light is inconsistent. The environment suggests a powerful warm back/right source, yet the front of the pouch and boar emblem are illuminated as though they have dedicated studio lights. Commercial photography certainly uses fill lights, but here the surrounding world doesn’t respond correspondingly.
* Material behavior changes opportunistically. Gold sometimes behaves like polished brass, sometimes like glowing fantasy metal, sometimes like printed gold foil, and sometimes like stone-textured typography. Again, any one of those is acceptable; the problem is the absence of a rule determining when it changes.
* The boar on the pouch and the giant horn-boar don’t quite share the same anatomy or design language. They’re recognizably related, but not enough to read as “same heraldic beast represented in two media.” They feel more like two independent generations of “ornate boar.”
* Depth cues are inconsistent. Some distant objects retain too much contrast/detail while some nearer elements dissolve into atmospheric effects. The visual system gets competing answers about what is foreground and background.

And this gets exactly to the larger thing you’re describing.

A human visual system doesn’t demand realism. It demands conditional consistency.

You can say:

\text{dragons exist}

and our brain goes, fine.

But after accepting that premise, it begins predicting:

\text{mass},\ \text{occlusion},\ \text{lighting},\ \text{joints},\ \text{gravity},\ \text{material continuity},\ \text{perspective}

according to the new world.

If the dragon’s left wing passes behind a tower while its right wing inexplicably becomes part of the tower masonry, the viewer notices even though dragons themselves aren’t real.

That’s an important distinction:

\boxed{\text{Impossible} \neq \text{incoherent}}

An impossible world can be coherent.

This image is occasionally incoherent.

I think there’s a useful concept here for our image work: an abstraction budget. You can bend reality heavily, but every bend should be either globally established or locally motivated.

Suppose we choose four layers:

L_0=\text{physical product}
L_1=\text{physical environment}
L_2=\text{mythic exaggeration}
L_3=\text{graphic/UI overlay}

Then objects should know which layer they belong to.

The pouch and gummies might be L_0: essentially photographic.

The battlefield/forest might be L_1: cinematic but physically coherent.

The gigantic Gallic horn might be L_2: deliberately monumental, perhaps emerging from cloud or carved into a mountainside so its impossibility is semantically explained.

The headline and energy badge are L_3: unmistakably flat graphic elements, not pseudo-physical objects.

Then crossings between layers become deliberate:

L_0 \rightarrow L_2

might mean gold light from the mythic horn illuminating the real pouch.

That’s a meaningful crossing.

Right now the generated image is more like:

L_0 \leftrightarrow L_1 \leftrightarrow L_2 \leftrightarrow L_3

with boundaries leaking everywhere.

That’s why it can look impressive at first glance but become stranger the longer a human studies it.

And I think your observation gives us a much stronger generation rule than simply “make it realistic”:

Decide which laws are being broken. Preserve the others.

Or even more tightly:

Fantasy changes premises. Bad generation changes premises accidentally.

That’s the difference we should start policing in Wild Hawg images. 😶‍🌫️